  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R52", "R8"],
      "pins": ["REG3.24"],
      "nets": ["NET0028", "+5V"],
      "observed": "The MIC26903YJL-TR buck feedback divider is drawn as R52 = 6k8 (top, to +5V) and R8 = 19k6 (bottom, to GND), with the midpoint NET0028 going to FB pin 24. The schematic note states Vout = 0.8*(1+R5/R6).",
      "expected": "For a 5 V output with the 0.8 V reference, the divider ratio must be Rtop/Rbottom = 5.25. With R8 = 19k6 the top resistor should be about 100 kΩ; with R52 = 6k8 the bottom resistor should be about 1.3 kΩ. The drawn 6k8/19k6 pair gives only about 1.08 V, far from the specified 5 V rail.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R52 6k8 ... R8 19k6 ... Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6) (Power sheet, MIC26903 feedback network)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "5 V rail: A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V. Design notes on this section: ... feedback divider relation Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V rail would regulate to roughly 1.1 V instead of 5 V, so the servo rail, GPS module, CH330 bridges and the 3.3 V LDO input would all be starved and the board would not function.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["U16"],
      "pins": ["U16.8"],
      "nets": ["+5.3V"],
      "observed": "The W25N01GVZEIG_TR NAND flash VCC pin 8 is tied to a rail labelled +5.3V.",
      "expected": "The W25N01GV is a 3.3 V NAND device and should be powered from the 3.3 V rail, not from a 5 V-class rail.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "+5.3V at U16 VCC pin 8 (FCC Peripherals sheet, FLASH Memory block)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "FCC-side FLASH | FCCPeripherals | Winbond W25N01GVZEIG_TR (1 Gb NAND)"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the label is truly +5.3V (or +5V), the flash is over-volted and may be damaged or draw excessive current; if it is a mislabelled 3.3 V net, the flash is simply not powered from the intended rail.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely consistent with the specification, but the MIC26903 5 V buck feedback divider (R52 = 6k8 / R8 = 19k6) does not produce 5 V with the stated 0.8 V reference, and the W25N01GV flash VCC appears tied to a +5.3V label rather than 3.3 V. The feedback-divider value mismatch is the strongest candidate anomaly."
}

